-- ============================================================================
-- PD2 ASSOCIATE DEEP SEED: Gas Lift Design, beginner tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE GAS COLUMN. A dead well is full of kill fluid, and
-- lifting it starts with knowing what the injection gas weighs on its way
-- down. Everything the later tiers do rests on a pressure read at a depth.
--
-- THE RESULT THIS TIER EXISTS FOR, AND THE COURSE BRIEF GOT IT BACKWARDS ON
-- THE FIRST DRAFT. The gradient grows with PRESSURE. Whether it grows with
-- DEPTH is a race, and on ordinary geotherms the temperature wins: on all
-- three published columns the local gradient FALLS with depth, by 2.02, 4.90
-- and 2.02 percent. Hold the same column at its wellhead temperature and it
-- RISES by 26.0, 43.4 and 10.1 percent. The isothermal control is the whole
-- argument, and the tier teaches the control rather than the story, because a
-- plausible physical account that names only one of two competing effects is
-- the commonest way to be confidently wrong.
--
-- It follows that the flat 0.02 psi/ft rule of thumb errs in BOTH directions,
-- reading low against a high pressure column whose local gradient is 1.99
-- times the rule and high against a low pressure one at 0.67 times it.
--
-- AN HONEST NEGATIVE RESULT, WHICH IS THE HARDER THING TO TEACH. This gas
-- column march is essentially exact: at the packer, 20 steps and 2000 steps
-- differ by 0.0036 psi, and the injection pressure curve carries a chord bias
-- of a ten-thousandth of a psi at 64 samples. The Cullender and Smith
-- truncation that dominates the neighbouring nodal course has NO counterpart
-- here. A programme that only ever reports defects is not measuring anything,
-- and a learner who leaves believing every numerical method is suspect has
-- learned the wrong thing.
--
-- THE SKILL IS TELLING THE TWO CASES APART, and the tier teaches it on THREE
-- references against ONE march. Against a fine reference the error quarters
-- every doubling and heads for zero. Against the textbook closed form it
-- PARKS at a floor refinement never touches. Against the engine's own
-- constant it converges to about 1e-9 psi. The marched pressures are identical
-- in all three columns. A residual that will not shrink is telling you that
-- you are comparing two DIFFERENT THINGS, and the job is to find which two:
-- here it was one constant written two ways, the textbook 0.01875 against the
-- engine's own AIR_MW over 144 R, agreeing to four significant figures and no
-- further.
--
-- The first version of that check printed one closed form alone and called a
-- still residual convergence, so it COULD NOT FAIL. Two lesson writers
-- refined the march, watched the residual park, and reported it instead of
-- writing a sentence around it.
-- ============================================================================

